  "text": "We are given a **3x3 determinant**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd we are told that the angles $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ satisfy the condition:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe are to **find the value of this determinant**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Recognize the structure of the matrix\n\nLet’s denote the matrix as:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is a **matrix of trigonometric functions of angles**. The determinant of this matrix is the quantity we are to compute.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use symmetry and trigonometric identities\n\nLet’s consider the trigonometric identities:\n\n- $\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1$\n- $\\cos x \\sin x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2x)$\n\nSo, the first column of the matrix is:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\alpha, \\cos^2 \\beta, \\cos^2 \\gamma\n$$\n\nThe second column is:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha, \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta, \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma\n$$\n\nThe third column is:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 \\alpha, \\sin^2 \\beta, \\sin^2 \\gamma\n$$\n\nSo the matrix is:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\alpha) & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\beta) & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\gamma) & \\sin^2 \\gamma\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is a **symmetric matrix** with rows corresponding to angles $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Try to simplify the determinant\n\nLet’s denote the matrix as:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet’s denote the rows as $R_1, R_2, R_3$.\n\nWe can try to **add rows together** or use **column operations** to simplify the determinant.\n\nLet’s try to compute the determinant directly.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Use the identity for $\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1$\n\nNote that the **sum of the entries in each row** is:\n\n- Row 1: $\\cos^2 \\alpha + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\alpha = 1 + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha$\n- Similarly for rows 2 and 3.\n\nSo each row sums to:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\alpha)\n$$\n\nBut this doesn't immediately help.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Try to simplify the determinant using row operations\n\nLet’s denote the matrix as:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet’s denote the rows as $R_1, R_2, R_3$.\n\nLet’s try to **subtract row 1 from row 2**, and **row 1 from row 3**.\n\nLet’s define:\n\n$$\nR_2' = R_2 - R_1 \\\\\nR_3' = R_3 - R_1\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nR_2' = (\\cos^2 \\beta - \\cos^2 \\alpha, \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha, \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin^2 \\alpha)\n$$\n\n$$\nR_3' = (\\cos^2 \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\alpha, \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha, \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\alpha)\n$$\n\nNow, the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\nM' = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\alpha\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the determinant of this new matrix.\n\nLet’s denote the new matrix as $M'$. Then:\n\n$$\n\\det(M) = \\det(M') = \\det\\left(\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\alpha\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the first row is unchanged.\n\nLet’s compute the determinant using the **first row expansion**.\n\nLet’s denote the determinant as:\n\n$$\n\\det(M) = \\cos^2 \\alpha \\cdot \\det\\left(\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\alpha\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right)\n$$\n$$\n- \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha \\cdot \\det\\left(\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\beta - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\beta - \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\alpha\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right)\n$$\n$$\n+ \\sin^2 \\alpha \\cdot \\det\\left(\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\beta - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right)\n$$\n\nThis is very complicated.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Try a different approach\n\nLet’s try to **factor the matrix** or recognize a pattern.\n\nLet’s note that:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x = \\frac{1 + \\cos(2x)}{2}, \\quad \\sin^2 x = \\frac{1 - \\cos(2x)}{2}, \\quad \\cos x \\sin x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nSo the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\alpha)}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\alpha) & \\frac{1 - \\cos(2\\alpha)}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\beta)}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\beta) & \\frac{1 - \\cos(2\\beta)}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\gamma)}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2\\gamma) & \\frac{1 - \\cos(2\\gamma)}{2}\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet’s denote $x = 2\\alpha$, $y = 2\\beta$, $z = 2\\gamma$. Then $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi \\Rightarrow x + y + z = 2\\pi$.\n\nSo now the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\frac{1 + \\cos x}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x & \\frac{1 - \\cos x}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos y}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin y & \\frac{1 - \\cos y}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos z}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin z & \\frac{1 - \\cos z}{2}\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, observe that this matrix is of the form:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}\n\\frac{1 + \\cos x}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x & \\frac{1 - \\cos x}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos y}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin y & \\frac{1 - \\cos y}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1 + \\cos z}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin z & \\frac{1 - \\cos z}{2}\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet’s now **add the first and third columns**.\n\nLet’s define:\n\n- $C_1 = \\frac{1 + \\cos x}{2}, \\frac{1 + \\cos y}{2}, \\frac{1 + \\cos z}{2}$\n- $C_3 = \\frac{1 - \\cos x}{2}, \\frac{1 - \\cos y}{2}, \\frac{1 - \\cos z}{2}$\n\nThen $C_1 + C_3 = \\frac{2}{2} = 1$, so the first and third columns sum to 1.\n\nSo, the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x \\\\\n1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin y & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin y \\\\\n1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin z & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin z\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWait — this is a **matrix with rows all equal to**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin x\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo the matrix is **rank 1**, and hence its **determinant is zero**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
